  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nIR currently depicts mainly clear skies into Monday morning \nwith some passing clouds through Sweetwater County pushing east \nby sunrise. Water vapor shows weak upper level southwest flow \nacross the CWA with the main finger of the PFJ well to the north\nsupporting a low off the Pacific northwest coast. This will \ndeepen with its southward progression Monday into Tuesday with \nincreasing divergence aloft. Seasonable temperatures and \nrelatively light or modest winds 10 to 20 mph through mid week \nexpected. A weak shortwave will propagate across the southwest \nflow Tuesday afternoon and evening that will bring isolated \nshower activity for higher terrain, mainly west of the Divide. \nLow coverage is expected ~20 percent with no significant impacts\nexpected. \n\nAs the aforementioned low starts to move east across southern\nCalifornia Wednesday, models indicate it becoming more of an \nopen and more progressive wave. This will then push along the \nsouthwest flow across the upper Rockies not of high of an impact\nwith its quicker movement. However, models are not in the best \nof agreement and QPF amounts fluctuate with each model run. \nWith this run, it has bumped up for mountain snow, especially \nfor western mountains but does include the Bighorns. With the \nseasonable to just above temperatures, expect valley/basin \nrain, even west of the Divide below 8-9kft. This will be the \nbiggest weather event to keep an eye on for headlines going \nforward for late Thursday into Friday, stretching into the \nweekend, all dependent on how quickly it progresses with its \neast to northeastward movement. Beyond that, induced ridging \nwill be short lived ahead of another low off the Pacific \nnorthwest extending in from the GOA that could be more impactful\nwith a more reinforced colder air and lower freezing levels. \nStay tuned for updated forecasts in the coming days, but enjoy \nthe quieter weather leading up to later in the work week.",
